"
\h0#preface|Preface|

This document describes \c{headers}, a tool for adding the SPDX header to
the source files of git repositories. For the command line interface refer
to the \l{headers(1)} man page.

\h1#intro|Introduction|

Every source file of an IW4x project starts with a header that names the
copyright holder and the license under which the file is distributed. For
example, in C++:

\
// Copyright (c) the IW4x authors (see the AUTHORS file).
// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-only WITH AdditionRef-IW4x-Exception-1.1
\

The holder, the IW4x authors, is a collective name that the \c{AUTHORS} file
of the repository defines (see the \c{authors} manual for details). The
license is given as an SPDX license expression, a standard notation that
tools can read as well as people. The expression above stands for the GNU
General Public License, version 3 only, together with the IW4x Linking
Exception. The exception is not on the SPDX License List and so it is
referred to with the \c{AdditionRef-} prefix that SPDX provides for such
cases.

The \c{headers} tool adds this header to the source files of a git
repository. It takes the license expression from the repository manifest
(where the license of the repository is already recorded) and writes the
header in the comment syntax of each file type.

Note that \c{headers} only writes the header into files that carry no
notice at all. A repository may contain files that are not ours to label
(such as a library that was copied into it) as well as files with a notice
of their own (perhaps from before the project adopted this header), and
inserting our header into such a file could make a false statement about
its license. If \c{headers} finds such files (or files of a type it does
not recognize), then it lists them and changes nothing until each of them
is either excluded or given the header by hand.

We begin with a tutorial introduction that adds the headers to a repository
with a copied library in it and then discuss how files are classified and
handled in more detail.

\h1#start|Getting Started|

Let's start with a repository that contains a small program along with a
copy of the zlib header that the program uses:

\
$ cd hello/
$ git ls-files
README.md
hello/buildfile
hello/hello.cxx
libs/zlib/zlib.h
manifest
\

The repository is a \c{build2} package and its manifest specifies the IW4x
license:

\
$ grep license manifest
license: other: GPL-3.0-only with IW4x Linking Exception 1.1
\

None of the files has the header yet, so let's see what \c{headers} makes
of this repository:

\
$ headers
error: files in '/src/hello' carry notices of their own
  info: libs/zlib/zlib.h
  info: exclude third-party files with -spdx in .gitattributes
  info: or add the header next to the notice by hand
\

While that didn't work out, it does make sense: \c{zlib.h} begins with the
copyright notice of the zlib authors and our header has no business being
there. Notice also that the files that do need the header, \c{hello.cxx}
and the \c{buildfile}, were not changed either. \c{headers} does not change
a repository until every file in it is accounted for.

Since zlib is not ours, we exclude it by unsetting the \c{spdx} attribute
for its directory in \c{.gitattributes} (we will discuss this in more
detail in \l{#exclude Excluding Files}):

\
$ echo 'libs/zlib/** -spdx' >>.gitattributes
$ git add .gitattributes
$ git commit -m 'Exclude zlib from SPDX headers'
\

And now let's try again:

\
$ headers

repository:   /src/hello
license:      GPL-3.0-only WITH AdditionRef-IW4x-Exception-1.1
source files: 2 (0 with the header)
excluded:     1

add the header to:
  hello/buildfile
  hello/hello.cxx

add the header to 2 files in '/src/hello'? [y/n]: y
\

This time we get a plan, with the license expression for the license
value of the manifest. Of the two source files neither has the header yet
and one file, \c{zlib.h}, is excluded. \c{README.md} and \c{manifest} are
not counted at all, since documentation and manifests need no header (see
\l{#types File Types} for details).

Once we answer \c{y}, \c{headers} adds the header to both files, each in
its own comment syntax, and commits them:

\
$ head -4 hello/hello.cxx
// Copyright (c) the IW4x authors (see the AUTHORS file).
// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-only WITH AdditionRef-IW4x-Exception-1.1

#include <iostream>

$ head -2 hello/buildfile
# Copyright (c) the IW4x authors (see the AUTHORS file).
# SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-only WITH AdditionRef-IW4x-Exception-1.1
\

The commit subject is \c{Add SPDX headers} and the commit contains these
files and nothing else, whatever else may be staged. If we run \c{headers}
again, it finds that every source file has the header and exits without
changing anything:

\
$ headers
every source file in '/src/hello' has the header
\

The options are the same as those of \c{mailmap} and \c{authors} (see
\l{headers(1)} for details) with one addition, \c{--license}, which we will
come back to in \l{#license The License Expression}.

\h1#types|File Types|

\c{headers} establishes the type of every file git tracks from its name and
extension. The type determines whether the file is a source file and, if
it is, which comment syntax its header is written in. For example, C++ and
CLI files take \c{//}, buildfiles and shell scripts take \c{#}, and HTML
takes \c{<!-- -->} (see \l{headers(1)} for the complete list).

Some files cannot carry a header (such as images, or JSON since it has no
comments) and others don't need one since they are not source code (such
as documentation and manifests). Such files are skipped and
don't count as source files (see \l{headers(1)} for the complete list).

The name of a file does not always give its type away and there are two
rules for the common cases where it doesn't. A template (\c{*.in}) has the
type of the file it is used to produce, so \c{hello.cxx.in} is a C++ file.
And a file of otherwise unknown type that starts with a shebang line is a
script and takes \c{#}.

A file that none of this applies to is of unknown type and \c{headers}
lists it and fails:

\
error: files of unknown type in '/src/hello'
  info: data.frob
  info: exclude them with -spdx in .gitattributes
\

The reason for this is that a file of a type nobody has considered may
well be source code and skipping it would leave it without a header with
nobody noticing. If this happens, then either the type is added to
\c{headers} or, if the file does not need the header, the file is
excluded.

\h1#exclude|Excluding Files|

A file is excluded if its \c{spdx} attribute is unset (in
\c{.gitattributes}, the same way as any other git attribute). For
example, to exclude a copied library as well as a single third-party
header:

\
# Third-party code (see LEGAL).
#
libs/zlib/** -spdx
lib/include/stb_truetype.hpp -spdx
\

Keeping the exclusions in \c{.gitattributes} means that they are versioned
and reviewed along with the files they apply to and that everyone who runs
\c{headers} on the repository gets the same result. Generated files that
are kept in the repository are excluded the same way.

Note that excluding a file only tells \c{headers} to leave it alone and
says nothing about the file's license. The place that explains under what
terms such files are distributed is the \c{LEGAL} file of the repository.

\h1#notices|Existing Notices|

Before writing the header into a source file, \c{headers} reads the file's
leading comment, that is, the comment lines (and any blank lines between
them) at the beginning of the file. If any of them mentions a copyright or
license, then the file already carries a notice and \c{headers} lists it
for review. Only the leading comment is considered, so a function called
\c{has_license()} further down is not taken for a notice.

A notice can belong to someone else, as is the case for a library that was
copied into the repository, and then the file is excluded (see
\l{#exclude Excluding Files}). Or it can be an older notice of our own, in
which case we add the header by hand next to it and leave the notice as it
is. \c{headers} accepts the header anywhere in the file, so it considers
the following file to have it:

\
// Copyright (c) 2015 IW4x Team.
//
// Copyright (c) the IW4x authors (see the AUTHORS file).
// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-only WITH AdditionRef-IW4x-Exception-1.1
\

Note that \c{headers} never removes or rewrites a notice, and this includes
a header of its own that it considers wrong. A header that lacks one of its
lines or names another license expression is an error that is listed with
the reason. Here is what this looks like in a repository licensed under
MIT:

\
error: files in '/src/hello' have a wrong header
  info: hello.cxx: license 'GPL-2.0-only', expected 'MIT'
\

\h1#architecture|How headers Works|

In this chapter we look at where the license expression comes from and at
how \c{headers} writes the header into a file.

\h#license|The License Expression|

The license expression comes from the \c{license} value of the manifest in
the repository root or, if the repository has several packages, from the
manifests of the packages listed in \c{packages.manifest} (in which case
they must all have the same license). The license value itself is described
in the \c{bpkg} manual (see \l{bpkg#manifest-package-license
\c{license}}) and \c{headers} turns it into an expression as follows.

A license in the SPDX scheme is already an SPDX license expression and is
used as is, without the comment that may follow it. For example,
\c{license: BSD-3-Clause ; Kept from upstream.} results in the
\c{BSD-3-Clause} expression. The deprecated aliases that \c{bpkg} still
recognizes, such as \c{GPLv3}, stand for their SPDX names.

The IW4x license is written in the \c{other} scheme, where names compare
case-insensitively, and it becomes the expression we saw in the
introduction. Any other name in the \c{other} scheme has no expression and
neither does a comma-separated list of names (all of which apply) or
multiple license values (these leave the choice of the license to the user
of the package). In all these
cases the expression has to be specified with \c{--license}:

\
$ headers --license GPL-3.0-only
\

The same option is used for a repository without a manifest (such as one
that is yet to be migrated to \c{build2}).

Taking the expression from the manifest also means that the headers of a
repository follow its license. For example, a repository whose contributors
have not agreed to the IW4x Linking Exception has a manifest that says
\c{GPL-3.0-only}, and its headers say the same.

\h#placement|Placement|

The header is written at the very beginning of the file, except for the
lines that have to stay first. The byte order mark (if any) stays first and
so does the shebang line (the system only recognizes it on the first
line). In HTML and XML the XML declaration and the document type
declaration stay first as well. A comment before \c{<!DOCTYPE html>}, for
example, would put a browser into its quirks mode.

The header is followed by an empty line, and any blank lines the file
started with are dropped so that there is exactly one. In a man page the
header is followed by an empty comment (\c{.\\\"}) since an empty line would
appear in the output.

The header is also written with the line endings of the file, so a file
with CRLF line endings keeps them. And a file that does not end with a
newline still does not end with one after the header is added.
"
